WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.520
k-nearest neighbor is instance-based or lazy&nbsp;
learning. That means that you get all the answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.200
in one big splash. If something changes in your&nbsp;
data then you have to rerun the algorithm from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.400
scratch. This might make it difficult to scale&nbsp;
these models because you're working with much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.240
more data at any one time. Sometimes you don't&nbsp;
want instance-based machine learning algorithms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.320
Instead, you'll want to see a continuous numerical&nbsp;
relationship between different parts of your data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.920
For that, you might use something&nbsp;
like regression analysis.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:45.120
Regression analysis looks at the relationship&nbsp;
between predictors and your outcome. Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.160
you'll hear predictors called input variables,&nbsp;
independent variables, or even regressors.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.760
Linear regression is one of the most common&nbsp;
types of machine learning algorithms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.200
With linear regression, you'll want to create a&nbsp;
straight line that shows the relationship between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.240
your predictor and the outcome. You'll want&nbsp;
to see all the different data points closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.840
gathered around the straight line. Then you&nbsp;
can make the best prediction of your outcome.&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.280
Let's think about how this might work.&nbsp;
Imagine you're an owner of an ice cream shop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.920
Over the last year, you've collected 20 days&nbsp;
of sales data. Then you went out and purchased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.920
the weather data for those days. So, with this&nbsp;
training data, you create a simple x and y-axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.760
diagram as a scatter plot. This is when you plot&nbsp;
your data along different parts of your diagram.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.800
Along the y-axis, you list the daily sales. And&nbsp;
along the x-axis, you put a temperature scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.760
Let's say it starts at 60 degrees and ends at 110&nbsp;
degrees so you plot all of your different data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:56.560
points on your diagram. Then you'll try to create&nbsp;
a line of best fit. To do this, you create a line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:03.120
that splits your data. This is usually called the&nbsp;
hyperplane. Sometimes you'll also hear this called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:09.120
the trend line. So, you can see from our scatter&nbsp;
plot diagram that there's a very clear trend line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.520
The more the temperature goes up, the greater your&nbsp;
ice cream sales. You can also see that there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.640
few times that you have data points that are well&nbsp;
outside the trend line. This could be because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.400
there was a festival or maybe just a lot of people&nbsp;
wanted ice cream on a cold day. If you have a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.040
of data points that are far away from the trend&nbsp;
line, then it will be much more difficult for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.720
to predict your ice cream sales. As it stands,&nbsp;
there aren't many outliers so you can use your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.680
linear regression to predict your sales.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:45.040
Let's look at a point in this diagram. Let's&nbsp;
say that all this week, the weather forecast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.200
says it will be around 95 degrees. Now we can&nbsp;
look at our hyperplane or trend line and try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.320
find the corresponding ice cream sales. You'll&nbsp;
see that you can expect to sell around $3,000.&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.760
As you can imagine, not just ice cream shop owners&nbsp;
are interested in using machine learning linear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.920
regression. In fact, if you have more data, it's&nbsp;
usually easier to make an accurate trend line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.800
One interesting about linear regression is&nbsp;
that there are some debate about whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.520
it's actually machine learning. There's some&nbsp;
truth to it because if you think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.800
the machine is not actually learning anything&nbsp;
new about the data. It's just using the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.160
to create a standard statistical model. It's&nbsp;
less about learning and more about predicting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:37.040
Either way, regression is a very popular way to&nbsp;
try and predict future behavior. The key is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:45.840
find the right predictors and look for some&nbsp;
type of linear connection with the outcome.

